@article{lu2020tipping,
  title = {{The Tipping Point of Medical Technology: Implications for the Postpandemic Era}},
  author = {Lu, Amy C. and Cannesson, Maxime P. and Kamdar, Nirav Vikram},
  journal = {Anesthesia \& Analgesia},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {131},
  number = {2},
  pages = {335--339},
  doi = {10.1213/ane.0000000000005040},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.1213/ane.0000000000005040},
  abstract = {See Article, p 340 The unprecedented challenges of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) have pushed the limits of medicine and health care over the tipping point of what we thought imaginable, forcing solutions to problems that previously mired rapid progress. Nowhere has this been more apparent than in technology, where both its accelerated advances and shortcomings have come into sharp contrast during the current pandemic. Whereas classic infection-control and public health measures were used during the severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) epidemic in 2003, COVID-19 provides the opportunity to explore the potential of new digital technologies, including big data analytics, artificial intelligence, blockchain technology, and the Internet of Things.1 Among the many available digital technologies, O’Reilly-Shah et al2 in this issue of Anesthesia \& Analgesia address not only the potential benefits but also the barriers to adopting health informatics for patient care during the COVID-19 pandemic.2 They review concerns around current gaps in technology, including data privacy and ethics, we well as data silos and sharing. They highlight the lack of data infrastructure and interoperability as barriers to patient care and public health efforts during the pandemic. A recent study similarly observed that barriers to public health agencies receiving hospital-level data on COVID-19 patients included the inability to electronically receive data, interface-related issues, difficulty extracting data from the electronic health record (EHR), and different vocabulary standards.3 O’Reilly Shah et al2 also highlight concrete examples of the pandemic pushing the creative edges of technology.2 Like other authors,4 they note the proliferation of clinical decision support tools such as best practice alerts, order sets, and dashboards designed to track real-time COVID-19 updates in a hospital. While O’Reilly-Shah et al2 review the advantages and gaps currently existing specifically in clinical informatics,2 technology―in all of its forms―is rapidly evolving to address the pandemic. The apparent transition to the postinitial surge phase of the COVID-19 pandemic serves as an inflection point to reflect on the potential technological contributions of anesthesiology and anesthesiologists. Considering the expansive array of digital technologies available to contemporary health care, we take the liberty in this current editorial to discuss the anesthesiologist’s optimal role in this broader arena. Indeed, equally relevant to any discussion on technological innovation are the creative nondigital solutions that have been implemented during the initial COVID-19 surge. Many of these were developed by necessity, such as alternative personal protective equipment (PPE) options and reprocessing and sterilization techniques for N95 respirator masks. Critical shortages of PPE-motivated amateurs and expert manufacturers to utilize 3-dimensional (3D) printing to create medical supplies like face shields, face masks, and nasal swabs. Novel medical technologies like intubation boxes to minimize aerosolization, splitting ventilators, and new severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) testing and treatment options are continually being developed. The specialty of anesthesiology has a strong track record of innovations in medical device technology―witnessed again with COVID-19. We posit, however, that the current pandemic offers a unique opportunity to contribute to emerging digital technologies that have not been conventionally considered part of the purview of anesthesiology. This includes expanding our roles in telehealth platforms and remote monitoring and surveillance in the inpatient and outpatient settings, thereby adding significant value to the continuum of care through perioperative medicine. As we expand these technologies, it will be incumbent upon us to apply equally innovative metrics to measure the clinical and quality outcomes of these interventions in the perioperative setting. TELEHEALTH VISITS AND REMOTE PATIENT MONITORING One of the most striking transformations during the current pandemic has been the rapid adoption of telehealth and telemedicine. Before the emergence of COVID-19, despite impressive advances in video and mobile technology, telehealth progress was stymied by highly restrictive stipulations by the US Centers for Medicare \& Medicaid Services (CMS), state boundaries, and poor reimbursement. The pandemic has spurred federal and state regulators to level the playing field by reducing barriers to telehealth adoption, including reimbursement parity laws and relaxing state geographical restrictions. The expansions also included several CMS emergency initiatives that expanded Medicare and Medicaid coverage, increasing the modalities and sites of coverage, such as personal residences, federally qualified health centers, and rural clinics.5 Congress passed relief bills allowing the US Department of Health and Human Services to approve telehealth grants, and the Federal Communications Commission started a COVID-19 Telehealth program. States followed suit with emergency directives to increase telehealth access and coverage. State-level, COVID-19–related shelter-in-place mandates prompted rapid implementation efforts highly relevant for the vast majority of specialties, including anesthesiology.6–8 As early adopters of telemedicine in the perioperative setting, our respective health systems realized a marked increase in opportunities to expand virtual preoperative consultation for patients. Facilitating the expansion of telehealth platforms simultaneously assisted staff and patients to adhere to statewide shelter-in-place mandates, reduce risk of exposure, and preserve PPE during the initial surges of the pandemic.9 The preoperative anesthesia clinics at our health systems rapidly adapted our existing telehealth workflow, allowing us to continue to risk stratify, optimize, and prioritize surgical cases when elective procedures were postponed. Anecdotally, one of our institutions implemented an increase from 30\% audiovideo visits to nearly 100\% within one business day. Our experiential learning from this radical transformation reflects what others have reported about their telehealth experiences during the pandemic.6 The challenges, opportunities, and solutions experienced by our preoperative medicine and pain medicine clinics during this transition are listed in Table 1. Table 1. - Lessons Learned From Rapid Telehealth Implementation for Anesthesiology-Led Clinics Challenge Opportunity and/or Resolution Reimbursement for virtual visits Temporary waiving of requirements with “Act” Patient’s lack of adequate Internet access or usable electronic devices Patient education and intake materials sent ahead of appointmentUse of additional modalities approved by the “Act” (eg, Apple FaceTime, Facebook Messenger Video Chat, Google Hangouts Video, or Skype) Enrolling patients into electronic health portal and access to virtual visits Patient education team increased outreach and enrollment efforts Access to interpreter services Partnered with hospital interpreter services for virtual visits Training and resources for virtual visits Remote access training and home laptops coordinated for clinic staff Shelter-in-place mandate and personal protective equipment conservation Preoperative and pain virtual visits offered to all patients Preoperative diagnostic testing (eg, laboratory testing, electrocardiogram, and/or imaging) Obtained prior medical records to limit new testingTesting deferred to morning of surgery when possibleCoordinated with primary care physician if needed before surgery Inability to obtain reliable remote physical examination and vital signs Airway examination conducted through virtual visitVital signs recorded from patient’s home devices if possibleSeveral in-person visits to auscultate new cardiac murmursConsideration of long-term facilitated telemedicine options Patient experience Initiated ongoing patient experience survey with preliminary positive results Staff experience Staff expressed ease of learning telehealth platform and satisfaction at work-from-home safety measures Abbreviation: “Act,” Coronavirus Preparedness and Response Supplemental Appropriations Act. The initial COVID-19 pandemic surge and shelter-in-place mandates reduced our capability to perform a conventional physical examination of our patients, leading to the creative application of remote patient monitoring (RPM) and surveillance technologies. In the ambulatory setting, we shifted our reliance on patient-entered outcome data to remote monitoring in several scenarios, including continuous glucose monitors for glycemic control of insulinoma patients; Bluetooth weight scales for patients with congestive heart failure; consumer actigraphs to evaluate activity levels; and Bluetooth-enabled blood pressure cuffs to titrate alpha-blockade of pheochromocytoma patients. Within the hospital, remote surveillance offers unique abilities to safety monitor patients in non–intensive care unit (ICU) settings while conserving scarce medical equipment and PPE. Safavi et al10 describe the prerequisites and limitations for proper remote surveillance so clinicians can identify patients at risk for physiological deterioration.10 Their technological blueprints illustrate the importance of electronic medical records (EMR)–based database structures and data lakes and the design challenges to assure reliable information access and interpretation. Knowledge gained from the inpatient setting has direct application for monitoring infectious disease outside the hospital using patient self-entered clinical data. For example, institutions across the country have implemented EHR-embedded tools to remotely monitor asymptomatic, COVID-positive patients at home11; and to detect physiological deterioration with remote monitoring (pulse oximetry and temperature), asynchronous questionnaires, and video visits to advise a return to the hospital or clinic only if a patient becomes febrile or dyspneic or exhibits oxygen desaturation.12 This progress made in COVID-19 outpatient remote surveillance will advance postdischarge follow-up in the perioperative setting. There is growing impetus for surgeon-anesthesiologist to collaboratively participate in the “hospital-at-home” paradigm.13,14 Improving effectiveness and efficiency through clinical pathways will also reduce hospital-acquired infections and other complications, thereby reducing length-of-stay―all of which are paramount as we define the “new normal” in perioperative care. Therefore, current successful experience with remotely triaging and managing asymptomatic COVID-19 patients can pave the way to widespread future RPM implementation in the postoperative discharge setting. Anesthesiologists are well-trained to triage and to manage postsurgical patients using RPM technologies, and can thus potentially serve as reliable touchpoints for the 7- or 14-day transition of care visit in the outpatient setting. Similar to EHR platforms, however, telehealth and RPM technologies have unresolved limitations. Issues of data privacy and sharing, along with the shortcomings of digital infrastructures exist in these areas. We cannot ignore the digital divide and inequity of adoption that can occur along racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic lines, including limited access in underserved areas or among vulnerable patient populations.15 Public policy must support sustainable reimbursement models for virtual health visits. Finally, robust outcomes research is necessary to assess the clinical effectiveness of these new technologies for patient care. While COVID-19 certainly has convinced some previous skeptics of the relevance and safety of telehealth, O’Reilly-Shah et al2 aptly remind us to identify and address its limitations. IMPLICATIONS ON THE DELIVERY OF CARE: REDESIGNING QUALITY AND CLINICAL METRICS TO REFLECT TECHNOLOGY PRE- AND POST-COVID O’Reilly-Shah et al2 reflect on the ethics and privacy challenges with data-sharing.2 Demonstrating and measuring improvement are equally relevant to the successful implementation and sustainability of any technological platform. Creating a robust and consistent framework in these areas will ensure that the impact of technological advances on the quality, safety, and access to care are validly measured. Certain types of technology, specifically, telehealth and telemedicine, have an assessment process recommended by national organizations, including the National Quality Forum (NQF), which could be extrapolated to measure the impact of other emerging technologies like predictive analytics and machine learning. The NQF has identified 3 factors most relevant to the adoption of technology: measuring its effect on quality outcomes, processes, and cost; selecting widely impactful quality measures; and using consistent definitions.16 The NQF also has defined essential categories for measuring telehealth as a means of care delivery, including access to care, financial impact to patients and their care providers, patient and clinician experience, and effectiveness of clinical and operational systems. Among these, the NQF suggested 6 priority areas: travel, timeliness of care, actionable information, impact of telehealth in providing evidence-based practices, patient empowerment, and care coordination. These NQF recommendations can serve as a guide in creating metrics for the impact of technology in anesthesiology and perioperative medicine. Using the quality domain as a framework to monitor the outcomes, access, and consistency of innovative technologies also has implications for how these modalities can be included in payment systems. For instance, the NQF has suggested incorporating telehealth and telemedicine into the Merit-Based Incentive Payment System (MIPS) with respect to providing expanded practice access and encouraging population health management. Each of the 9 MIPS improvement activity (IA) subcategories can be used to measure the impact of technology in anesthesiology and perioperative medicine (Table 2). Table 2. - Technology Improvement Activities for Anesthesiology and Perioperative Medicine IA Subcategory Example Expanded practice access Expanded telehealth hours in preoperative anesthesia clinic Population management Use of QCDR to track population outcomes during pre- and postoperative consultation Care coordination Implementing care coordination and transitions of care planning in preoperative, postoperative, and postdischarge settings Beneficiary engagement Use of electronic patient portals for preoperative optimization and patient education Patient safety and practice assessment Use of risk assessment tools (eg, NSQIP Surgical Risk Calculator) Participation in APM Hospital-at-home model Achieving health equity Measuring access to virtual visits in preoperative and postdischarge settings for patients from different geographical locations Emergency response and preparedness COVID-19 preparation as crisis care situation and public health emergency Integrated behavioral and mental health Smoking cessation interventions in preoperative clinic Abbreviations: APM, alternative payment model; COVID-19, coronavirus disease 2019; IA, improvement activity; NSQIP, National Surgical Quality Improvement Program; QCDR, Qualified Clinical Data Registry. While the COVID-19 pandemic accelerated technological innovation and facilitated easing of existing regulations for clinicians and federal technological oversight, it does not absolve practitioners from thoughtful analysis of the impact of new technology on care delivery. We advocate using consistent quality and clinical outcome measures in evaluating any technological platform and suggest how these can be easily applied to anesthesiology and perioperative medicine. Establishing a uniform framework will ensure addressing the ethical and health equity implications of technology as we chart a new course after the initial COVID-19 surge. ADVANCING PERIOPERATIVE MEDICINE AND VALUE-BASED CARE THROUGH TECHNOLOGICAL INNOVATION In the inevitable post-COVID pandemic era, what is the anesthesiologist’s role in technology as our health care systems and other major stakeholders define the “new normal?” Is this our opportunity to take a legitimate seat at the table of state and national discussions on value-based care and population health management strategies using our understanding of the continuum of care from the preoperative phase through the postdischarge phase? We will need to consider the optimal role of technology in addressing the currently accrued, and likely future ebbing and flowing “care debt” of deferred surgical treatment due to canceled elective procedures, as well as deferred medical conditions that worsen and require emergency procedural intervention. Others have also suggested this window as a launching point for discussion on value-based care approaches in surgical and perioperative team-based settings.13 Working collaboratively will incorporate all members of a multidisciplinary team, including surgeons, anesthesiologists, nurses, physical therapists, and others, to embrace care delivery models that promote high value and efficient clinical care pathways and empower patients and caregivers through their coproduction and increased use of patient-reported outcomes. Future innovation of technological platforms will allow decentralized care delivery through virtual pre- and postoperative appointments and the growth of home-based care and rehabilitation. As anesthesiologists, we are uniquely positioned to add meaningful value to this discussion. We are not only able to monitor and treat continuously changing physiologic parameters but also to adapt to ever-evolving environments. These abilities contribute new dimensions to future care delivery models. Leveraging our specialty’s strengths in technology, hemodynamic monitoring, and predictive analytics provides the platform to redesign and advance our profession and perioperative medicine after the initial COVID-19 surge and beyond. The tipping point has arrived, technology will certainly advance, and adoption is sure to generate discontent. It is our duty and calling to embrace these frontiers and opportunities in the name of both invention and progress. DISCLOSURES Name: Amy C. Lu, MD, MPH. Contribution: This author helped draft the article and with the final approval of the manuscript. Conflicts of Interest: None. Name: Maxime Cannesson, MD, PhD. Contribution: This author helped draft the article and with the final approval of the manuscript. Conflicts of Interest: M. Cannesson is a consultant for Edwards Lifesciences (Irvine, CA) and Masimo Corp (Irvine, CA), and has funded research from Edwards Lifesciences and Masimo Corp. He is also the founder of Sironis, and he owns patents and receives royalties for closed-loop hemodynamic management technologies that have been licensed to Edwards Lifesciences. Name: Nirav Kamdar, MD, MPP, MBA. Contribution: This author helped draft the article and with the final approval of the manuscript. Conflicts of Interest: N. Kamdar is a scientific advisor to HAI Solutions LLC (Carlsbad, CA), and Heartcloud Inc (Irvine, CA). This manuscript was handled by: Thomas R. Vetter, MD, MPH.},
  keywords = {04_scenario_quality; candidate: patent analysis scenario planning technology},
  note = {OpenAlex W3032940667; cited_by 15; abstract_source openalex}
}

@article{weiss2012agnotology,
  title = {{Agnotology: How can we handle what we don't know in a knowing way?}},
  author = {Weiss, Kenneth M.},
  journal = {Evolutionary Anthropology Issues News and Reviews},
  year = {2012},
  volume = {21},
  number = {3},
  pages = {96--100},
  doi = {10.1002/evan.21303},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.1002/evan.21303},
  abstract = {Thanks to Plato, we have a record of what Socrates (Fig. 1), one of the most influential thinkers in human history, thought and said. In his peripatetic instruction, this professor-without-walls made two proclamations that are particularly worth considering. He said "the unexamined life is not worth living." That's music to the ears of any scientist, because examining the nature of life is what we do every day. We try to be as systematic as we can about generating knowledge, but Socrates said something else that's important: "As for me, all I know is that I know nothing." What he meant, in broad terms, was that it was through exploration that one gains knowledge, whittling away at what we don't know. But how do we know what we don't know? Socrates (ca.470-399 BC): Thinking about thinking. Source: ClipartEtc, etc.usf. edu. A field of scholarship called agnotology (see Wikipedia and Refs. 1,2) deals with this question. Robert Proctor, a Stanford historian, coined the term. His major focus has been how real-world facts can be manipulated or ignorance actually generated when information is distorted or obscured by special interests, as exemplified by tobacco companies' fight to prevent the evil leaf from being controlled, if not banned.3 Some anthropologists focus on how culture affects what we know or even what we choose to study, whether we're aware of that choice or not. They sometimes act as if the real world doesn't actually exist or can't be studied objectively. But even if we stay grounded in reality, truth involves more than unambiguous facts. Facts have many facets. Many are unambiguous. But what do we do if we don't have certainty, as occurs at the research frontier of any science? We sometimes just pass on such things. We simply do not know how many planets exist, how many Neandertals roamed the European landscape, or (dare I say it?) whether string theory is more than a fantasy devised to keep physicists off the streets. Evolutionary questions are particularly vulnerable to incomplete knowledge because the direct fossil and geological evidence is fragmentary, and the key events were unique and occurred in the rarely observable past. Evolutionary inferences are sometimes based on comparative study of present-day life, but they necessarily rest on assumptions that in some ways aren't provable even in principle. That can make our inferences somewhat circular.4, 5 Rather than make flat-out admissions of ignorance, we normally view our job as that of experts who go beyond what is securely known to research what isn't known, and to convey the results to our eager students or readers. But we should ask the agnotological question: How can we convey what we don't know? A typical explanation goes something like this: "We don't really know the answer, but research has shown that [blah, blah], so that our best guess is [such and such]." This may be a wholly acceptable way to explain what we're aware that we don't know, but how do we acknowledge, much less be specific about, the unknown unknowns? In fact, it's often far from clear to whom "our" refers, what "best" means, or even what a "guess" is. Let's take a look at what "we" (whoever we are) mean by such words. Scientific statements are intended to account for the world in causal terms. We don't accept effects without causes: "And God said 'Let there be light'" conveys no scientific information. Such claims belong to religion or mysticism. Nor are we happy just with description, which we denigrate as "Victorian beetle collecting" rather than science. We can enumerate the different shapes and colors of people around the world, but what we want to know as scientists is, in some rigorous sense, how much variation there is, how it is distributed, and how it came about. With uncertainty, there is usually more than one plausible scientifically legitimate explanation.6 There may be contentious factions, like those battling for string theory versus classical physics or neutralism versus Darwinism in evolutionary biology. As a result, "our best guess" usually asserts "my" view and that of my explanatory tribe. Even when there is only a single prevailing view, it is unclear how one would know that it is correct, because that implies an unchangeable explanation. Even such a statement as that parallel lines never intersect, which seems to be an unassailably cosmic truth of existence, is only true on the surface of flat, infinite, and hence non¬existent frames of reference. Exceptions exist, such as parallels of longitude on spherical surfaces, which cross twice, at each pole. Since nobody is completely knowledgeable in any field of science, a speaker invoking "our" is usually summarizing an understanding of what other people have done rather than direct personal experience, echoing some sort of current consensus that would, in fact, be almost impossible to ascertain without, say, polling scientists. But we don't want to do that, because science is supposed to be about natural truth, not majority opinion. We want to be right, not just more numerous than our opponents. Yet history shows that majority views are often or even usually wrong. Even though we may sincerely feel that we accept negative evidence, which might falsify our hypothesis, when it arises, views are maintained in the presence of inconsistent facts, sometimes even by simply ignoring those facts. Perhaps the most relevant examples are Darwin's clinging to his theory in the face of problems with the estimated age of the earth7 or with an idea of inheritance, which he called pangenesis,8 and which was quickly shown to be wrong. In a purely deterministic causal world, if we know current conditions perfectly we can make perfect predictions of future outcomes.9 But we rarely have perfect measurement and, from what we think we know, the world isn't perfectly deterministic even if we did. As a result, predictions are almost never perfectly borne out. This forces us to couch even assumed certainties in terms of uncertainty in some way, which complicates the meaning of explanations. Let's take an example. We know that humans are normally diploid, except for mitochondria and X- and Y-chromosomes. Just like Mendel's peas, we have two copies of each gene, of which one is randomly transmitted to each sperm or egg that we shed to form the next generation. By "randomly" we mean that each of the copies will be transmitted 50\% of the time, a value known as the segregation proportion. But in real life, this value isn't uniformly observed and, indeed, is impossible in families with odd numbers of children. Suppose, for example, an individual has a G and a T in a corresponding nucleotide position in his or her two copies of a gene and we observe that 33\% of that person's offspring have inherited the G, while 67\% have inherited the T. How, if we feel sure of our Mendelian assumptions, do we interpret this? The mathematically best estimate we can make of the segregation proportion for the G from these observations is 33\%, not 50\%. Despite these facts, so convinced are we that we know the probability of transmission is 50\% that we actually take the data as supporting the theory. We do that by a subtle trick, using statistical theory to argue that in a given family 33\% is not "significantly" different from 50\%. We insist on this, even if we can't show it by directly observing all the molecules that go into the bumping of a given chromosome into this or that specific egg or sperm cell. This is exceedingly strange. "Our best guess" is different from what the actual evidence says should be our best guess. In fact, there are known examples in which genetic variants impair a sperm or egg so that the transmission really is different from 50\% by the same kind of significance test (this is called meiotic drive). So it is actually not true that Mendelian proportions are always what we expect to see. But we call such facts 'anomalies', which forces them to fit a preexisting theory, the way the mythical Procrustes altered his victims to make them fit his bed (Fig. 2). Procrustes. Anyone can be "fitted" into his "bed"…with a little help from the axe. Source: Wikimedia Commons. This Mendelian example shows how we decide what our "best guess" is by formulating a theory and establishing criteria that enable us to evaluate the theory in light of imperfect data. It is a theory of the separation of chromosomes, when no biological selection or other mechanism prefers one to the other. We tacitly assume that the probability with a parameter (50\%) that we can specify reflects the underlying molecular processes that we predict yield an objective, but probabilistic rather than deterministic cause, and that we can ascertain only probabilistically from samples of data. But at the end, we use the subjective cutoff criterion of significance level to show that our idea is correct.10 This is a common way to test a theory. It's based on the concept of outcomes of repeatable events. But it's a judgment that's not as rigorous as it seems on the surface. How different would results have to be before we rejected the theory as the scientific method is designed to do? If we diligently collected data on segregation proportions, the way Mendel did with his peas, how different from 50\% would results, even statistically significant results, have to be before we abandoned ship? What if, with large amounts of data, we found that segregation proportions were consistently, say, 53\%, and by our chosen test that this was "significantly" different from 50\%? How hard would we work to try to show why, despite the evidence, this anomaly occurred rather than the "correct" 50\%? It's easy to see many reasons why showing that wouldn't be easy (and indeed, it's related to controversies about whether Mendel cheated or not11). In fact, there's a precedent for this kind of predicament: In most populations, about 105 boys are born to every 100 girls, even though, from our XX/XY sex-determining chromosomes and from evolutionary theory, the sex ratio "should" be 50:50. Many explanations, often rather contorted, have been offered for this repeatedly observed, statistically significant fact (for example, see Wikipedia: human sex ratio). Agnotological problems about doubt are common in evolutionary anthropology, as is shown when we look at some typical types of explanation. Evolution is a process leading to divergent branching from common ancestry. One thing we want to do is reconstruct the timing and pattern of that shared ancestry. Here, we have a general kind of theory called population genetics, which provides a guide. If our understanding of evolution is anywhere near the truth, as it clearly seems to be, any set of species must have a common origin. The subsequent occurrence of mutations leads to an accumulation of genetic divergence among isolated lineages. We use the amount of divergence to estimate the time since that isolation was established; that is, we're not testing the assumption of common ancestry itself. The analysis leads to statements such as: "Our best guess" is that humans and chimpanzees "probably" had common ancestry about 6 million years ago (mya) (www.timetree.org). Here, we can put a specific kind of meaning on the statement. We stipulate a population genetic model of population sizes, mutation rates, and other theoretical aspects of frequency change in finite populations. Based on those assumptions, we estimate the most probable (that is, the most statistically likely) time since common ancestry. But here we face a problem in interpreting the result. Real evolution happened only once, but independent runs of the same probabilistic evolutionary process would yield somewhat different results, just as Mendelian segregation does. So we again use statistical theory, treating evolutionary events as if they were in fact repeatable, to put confidence limits around the estimate, which is only as good as the assumed model it's based on, to specify a range of what a best guess means. In the case of the human-chimp split, the limits are roughly between 5 and 8 million years. Even this rather substantial 3 million year range requires more, and less testable, assumptions than does Mendelian segregation. Even the choice of what confidence limits to use (99\%?, 95\%?, 90\%?, 80\%?) is a subjective one, for which there are conventions but no rules. That's no secret, and at least has a kind of specifiable precision, given the choice of data and assumptions in constructing the estimates. But no matter how sincerely they are made, those assumptions are judgments that can wildly affect the estimated dates.12-14 The evolutionary interpretation of morphological traits of fossils involves both function and relationship, and its agnotology becomes more interesting. For example, we typically employ functional comparisons among living related species, making the evolutionary assumption of relationship and extrapolating current function to the unobservable past. As in recent examples, we often hear or make statements such as that the hand of the Australopithecus sediba showed that they used tools,15 or that a fossil's dentition showed us what it ate or even that it must have cooked its food.16 Sometimes, we qualify that by saying the traits "suggest" or were "possibly" or "probably" what we assert. The latter versions sounds more reasonable and measured than the former, but are they really all that different? Suppose we believe—whatever that means scientifically—based on functional anatomical considerations, that fossil hands physically able to grasp tools probably evolved by adaptive natural selection for that purpose. It seems obvious what we mean, but if you think carefully it's not so clear. In reality, we're constrained to surmise about the past. But scientific statements should be verifiable, at least in principle. So suppose that to seek direct proof, we could jump on a time machine and visit the African sites at the actual time when the evolutionary events leading to tool-using hands were thought to have occurred. Suppose we observe our specific future-fossil specimen in the flesh and saw it using tools. Would that prove our evolutionary causal assertion? I don't think so. The world of science is paved with good intentions when correlations are equated with causation. The fact that the hypothesized behavior did in fact take place does not, in any sense, mean that it was genetically evolved as a consequence of selection specifically for that function. That just as specifically means not only that those who used tools systematically had more children, but that this was a result of tool-related genetic variants carried by the tool-user, because those are the only, and necessary, criteria for natural selection. Since sediba already had utile hands, for example, whether or not they were using tools is irrelevant to the adaptive argument because the relevant selection would already have taken place in their distant ancestry. So we'd have to transport even farther back. How far? The rudiments of the "best guess" selective scenario would, if evolutionary theory is correct, have been so slow and gradual as to be undemonstrable with any statistically reliable evidence (our criterion for inference from imperfect data) in any local African australopith-ancestral band. Initially, the advantage of rudimentary tool use, which we know chimpanzees also have, would have been small if conferred on a barely opposable thumb, with its muscle and neurological basis, embryological development, and the other fingers creeping toward some tool-related properties as well. Need we tally every time any hominid picked up a stick or threw a rock? Would we need only to visit one local band or would we have to travel around all over the African continent, tallying every grasping, prying, or throwing act and how many progeny the actor had during its entire life? Would we have any reason to expect that all the components of utile hands were present and being selected at the same time? Even counting the kids spawned by rock-throwers wouldn't be enough because Darwinian theory would force us to do the same for every gradation of tossing power in each population at each time in this long adaptive history since, as Darwin put it in the Origin of Species, selection detects even the tiniest of variants in relevant talent. And even then we'd have to show that rock-throwers' sexual success was actually a consequence of tool use, not some other aspect of gracile fingers and grasping thumbs, and that the variation in success correlated with genetic variation. Those digits might have been used initially or even systematically for some entirely different reason—say, being dexterous at various sexually titillating activities, the effect of which could be far more directly related to reproductive success than throwing rocks at wildebeests or berry bushes. Then, purely as a bonus, after a satisfying coupling, the creatures used their hands to make dinner. Does this prurient suggestion seem preposterous? If so, how can you show that it's more so than other explanations that are on offer? Clearly, what initially seems like a simple, persuasive explanation for a trait is far from simple. Why, then, are we so confident in offering adaptive functional explanations? Let's forget "proof." In what sense are those explanations "probable" or our "best guess" as opposed to our subjectively favored opinion among various others in the face of uncertainty? Suppose we decide to rescue ourselves with seemingly more rigorous population genetics, then look for genetic evidence of selection in genes that we know are related to, say, hand development during embryogenesis. This sounds quite sensible, but if you look at the population genetics models you'll see many different assumptions that typically aren't testable. First of all, what we look for are parts of the genome that reflect signatures of selection. There are various tests,17 but they generally search for reduced variation in areas of the genome that have been subjected to adaptive selection, favoring good variants but removing other variation from the population. Note that the test now is only a test of relative variation, regardless of the reason. The specific idea that some genes will vary less because of selection is simple but far less testable in terms of causation or the reason for the selection than are the statistical models discussed earlier. Most tests are not sensitive to creepingly slow, distantly past selection. This is especially true, given that complex traits like hands involve contributions from many genes, so that the selective intensity is spread across them to reduce their individual detectability.4, 17 How can we fancy that we can construct reliable adaptive scenarios with such estimates, even if the models tested are "plausible"? Just to show one aspect of the uncertainties, and whether time are as as they may from the human-chimp common to our of about 8 to or only 5 to using the confidence limits earlier. Evolutionary genetic change and adaptive natural selection, on population and and the of of specifiable and Would we really reconstruct the of the opposable and such with the same and population genetic explanations if it rather than seem like so it must something agnotological that such a acceptable range no to our explanation. The range and are for example, to be in to the evolution of such traits in population genetic terms and make of the to this even suppose tools had been found at the same as the Does that prove that the were using or making the We might say but in on our "best how can we evaluate that versus the that the was used by some other like a true to the future fossil or its This seems like without on the actual facts of the fossil specimen and its In a sense, we make based on evidence we don't have than if we could actually How far fro