@article{anon2025award,
  title = {{AWARD ABSTRACT}},
  journal = {Journal of Cytology},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {42},
  number = {Suppl 1},
  pages = {S74--S80},
  doi = {10.4103/joc.joc_149_25},
  url = {https://doi.org/10.4103/joc.joc_149_25},
  abstract = {NALINI BAI THAKAR AWARD 1. STUDY OF MICRONUCLEAR FREQUENCY AND ITS CORRELATION WITH CYTOLOGY, HISTOPATHOLOGY, AND IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY (p53 AND p16) IN ORAL POTENTIALLY MALIGNANT DISORDERS AND ORAL SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA: AN OBSERVATIONAL STUDY Asmita Chakrabarti, Moumita Maiti, Pranabashish Banerjee, Gopinath Barui; Department of Pathology, Nil Ratan Sircar Medical College and Hospital, Kolkata, West Bengal, India Introduction: Oral potentially malignant disorders (OPMDs) and oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) remain major health challenges in India, where delayed diagnosis contributes to poor outcomes. Micronuclear (Mn) frequency in exfoliated buccal epithelial cells is a simple marker of genomic instability and may serve as a useful adjunct in the early detection of dysplasia and malignancy. Aims: The present study evaluated Mn frequency in buccal smears of OPMDs and OSCC and examined its correlation with cytological and histological evidence of dysplasia and malignancy, as well as with immunohistochemistry (IHC) findings of p53 and p16. Materials and Methods: A descriptive, cross-sectional, observational study was conducted on 100 patients (56 OPMDs, 44 OSCC). All underwent scrape cytology followed by biopsy. Cytology and histology were assessed for dysplasia and malignancy using WHO criteria. Mn frequency per 100 cells was counted according to Tolbert’s criteria. IHC for p53 and p16 was performed on 64 biopsy samples. Results: Cytology and histology detected dysplasia in 55.4\% and 69.6\% of OPMDs respectively. Mn frequency was significantly higher in dysplastic versus non-dysplastic lesions in both cytology [2 (2-3)] vs. 1 (0-1)] as well as histology [2 (1-3) vs. 1 (0-1)]; (P < 0.05). In OPMDs, Mn frequency correlated with p53 positivity [2 (1.75-3)]; (P = 0.03) and showed a near significant association with p16 expression (P = 0.051). In OSCC, Mn counts were higher in cytologically malignant smears [2 (2-3)]; (P = 0.033). Mn frequency showed significant association with p16 expression (P = 0.007) but association with p53 was not significant (P = 0.259). Cytology demonstrated sensitivity of 66.7\% to detect dysplasia in OPMDs and 95.5\% to identify malignancy in OSCC. ROC analysis of OPMDs and OSCC showed good diagnostic accuracy of Mn frequency in OPMDs and OSCC in predicting dysplasia and malignancy respectively. Conclusion: Mn frequency is a simple, non-invasive biomarker for detecting oral epithelial dysplasia and early oral malignancy. Its association with p53 and p16 highlights its potential as a marker of genomic instability, complementing cytology in early detection and risk stratification. 2. DIAGNOSTIC UTILITY OF NUCLEAR MORPHOMETRY IN FINE NEEDLE ASPIRATES OF BREAST LESIONS Bishakha Deb, Sushma Ramraje, Megha Kinake; Department of Pathology, Grant Government Medical College and Sir JJ group of Hospitals, Mumbai, Maharashtra, India Introduction: Fine Needle Aspiration Cytology (FNAC) is a routine diagnostic modality for breast lesions but is subject to interpretive subjectivity, especially in the gray zone cases between benign and malignant lesions. Nuclear morphometry using computerised image analysis may enhance diagnostic accuracy by providing objective measurements of the FNAC smears. Aim: To evaluate the diagnostic utility of computerised nuclear morphometric assessment of FNAC smears in distinguishing benign and malignant breast lesions. Materials and Methods: A cross sectional study was conducted at a tertiary care teaching hospital over a period of 18 months. A total of 94 breast FNAC cases with subsequent histological diagnoses were included in the study, classified per the International Academy of Cytologists Yokohama System 2020 and further studied using Computerised Image Morphometry software (Morphonuclei v6.1). 50 nuclei per case were studied and the following 8 nuclear morphometric parameters analysed digitally: Nuclear perimeter (Henceforth referred to as the Perimeter), Nuclear perimeter Area of the cell, Maximum radius of the nucleus, Nuclear density, Nuclear area, Nuclear Circularity Nuclear elongation, Chromatin texture Statistical analysis was done using SPSS v29 software and included the Independent T – test and ANOVA. Observations and Results: Nuclear morphometric parameters specifically nuclear perimeter, area of the cell, maximum radius of the nucleus and nuclear density significantly differentiated between benign and malignant categories (P < 0.05). Chromatin texture abnormalities increased from benign to malignant lesions. Conclusion: Computerised nuclear morphometry on FNAC is a promising adjunct for improving objectivity particularly in diagnostically challenging cases. Further, it shows potential for integrating artificial intelligence with routine cytopathology for reporting of breast lesions and mass screening of malignancies. 3. ENHANCING ZIEHL-NEELSEN CYTOSMEAR TRIAGE WITH RESIDUAL ATTENTION AND CHANNEL FUSION Lavleen Singh1, Venkateswaran K Iyer1, Deychen Myes2, Sherrin Jacob1, Partha Pratim Roy3, Aparajita Khan4, Parshiv Kapoor5, Yash Bansal5; 1Department of Pathology, All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), New Delhi, India, 2Department of Pathology, AIIMS-CAPFIMS, New Delhi, India, 3Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Indian Institute of Technology Roorkee, Roorkee, Uttarakhand, India, 4Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Indian Institute of Technology (BHU) Varanasi, Varanasi, Uttar Pradesh, India, 5Department of Biosciences and Bioengineering, Indian Institute of Technology Roorkee, Roorkee, Uttarakhand, India Introduction: Manual screening of Ziehl–Neelsen (ZN) cytomears for acid-fast bacilli (AFB) is labor intensive and prone to fatigue-related oversight, especially when bacilli are sparse and stain variability is high To reduce review time while maintaining diagnostic sensitivity, we propose an AI triage system that prioritizes bacillary morphology (shape, edges) over color alone. Methods: Our framework, MorphoChanNet, analyzes 80 × 80 patches using a six-channel input: lightness, saturation, and value (LSV) plus their Sobel edge maps. Channels are selected based on reconstruction fidelity, ensuring preservation of bacillary contours across staining conditions. Features are processed through residual attention blocks and fused using reconstruction-guided channel weighting. Training employs a focal loss variant with added penalty for false positives, limiting artifact-driven alerts. A curated dataset of 200 annotated ZN images (100 AFB-positive, 100 AFB-negative; 1368 × 776 px) supported training and evaluation. Hard negatives were sampled from background, overstains, stain transitions, and bacillus-like debris. Interpretability was assessed via Grad-CAM. Workload was quantified with review fraction (RF), the proportion of patches flagged for pathologist review at high recall. Results: On held-out data, MorphoChanNet achieved F1 ≈ 97.2\% and accuracy ≈97.6\%, matching or exceeding deeper networks with fewer parameters. Grad-CAM localized to slender, rod-like structures, supporting face validity. At sensitivity-tuned thresholds, review fraction was reduced to a minority of patches, indicating practical workload savings. Discussion and Conclusions: By addressing stain variation, artifact confusion, and computational constraints MorphoChanNet translates accuracy into reduced screening burden. It enables pre-filtering of low-yield areas while preserving sensitivity, supporting adoption in high-volume and resource-limited labs. Future work will extend to pixel-wise localization and prospective workflow validation. 4. TRANSFER LEARNING IN CONVOLUTIONAL NEURAL NETWORK TO DIFFERENTIATE FOLLICULAR ADENOMA VERSUS FOLLICULAR CARCINOMA OF THYROID ON ASPIRATION CYTOLOGY MATERIAL Dipanwita Biswas1, Pranab Dey2, Rajiv Savala1; 1Post Graduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India, 2Department of Cytology and Gynecologic Pathology, Post Graduate Institute of Medical Education and Research, Chandigarh, India, Currently Professor of Oncopathology, Homi Bhabha Cancer Hospital and Research Centre (Tata Memorial Center), New Chandigarh, India Aims and Objectives: In the present paper, we tried to build a basic convolutional neural network model and a transfer learning model on it to distinguish follicular adenoma (FA) and follicular carcinoma (FC) of thyroid in fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC). Materials and Methods: We selected histopathology proven cases of FA (25 cases) and FC (29 cases). In each case, the best May-Grünwald-Giemsa-stained smear was selected and 10 to 11 representative microphotographs were taken with Olympus microscope (Olympus DP74) in 40X objectives from the most representative areas of the smear. There was total 347 microphotographs in FA and 229 microphotographs of FC. The images were divided into training (406 images, 70\%), validation (89 images, 15\%) and test set (81 images,15\%). The basic (scratch) model of convolutional neural network (CNN) was built in Python 3.11.11. We subsequently also used a transfer learning model by a pre-trained MobileNetV2 in the same images. We ran the model in Jupyter notebook for 15 epochs with 13 steps. Results: The sensitivity and specificity of the base model were 72.41\% and 88.46\% respectively. The Accuracy, precision and F1 score were 82.71\%, 77.78\% and 75.00\%. The area under the curve (AUC) of Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) was 0.85. The sensitivity and specificity of transfer learning model are 82.76\% and 92.31\% respectively. The Accuracy, precision and F1 score were 88.89\%, 85.71\% and 84.21\%. The area under the curve (AUC) of Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) is 0.94.Conclusions: We built a CNN base model and transfer learning model (MobileNetV2) and successfully distinguished FA and FC in thyroid FNAC. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study of CNN in thyroid follicular tumours. Keywords: Artificial neural network, CNN, Follicular tumour, Thyroid, Transfer learning 5. EGFR MUTATION, EGFR AND VEGF EXPRESSION IN FINE NEEDLE ASPIRATION CYTOLOGY SAMPLES IN METASTATIC ORAL SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA Jennifer Kimnuntluangi1, Meeta Singh1, Nidhi Verma1, Arun Kumar Rathi2, Binita Goswami3; 1Department of Pathology, Maulana Azad Medical College, New Delhi, India, 2Department of Radiotherapy, Maulana Azad Medical College, New Delhi, India, 3Department of Biochemistry, Maulana Azad Medical College, New Delhi, India Background: Oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) is a major health burden in India, with most patients presenting at advanced stages and poor survival. Epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) are important in tumor growth and angiogenesis, and their expression may influence prognosis. Fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) offers a minimally invasive method for diagnosis and biomarker evaluation. Aim: To assess EGFR mutations and EGFR/VEGF expression in FNAC samples of lymph nodes from metastatic OSCC and correlate findings with therapy response. Methods: A one-year prospective study of 117 metastatic SCC cases (61 oral cavity, 56 other/unknown) was conducted; 12 oral cases were excluded due to inadequate CB, and histopathology was available for 31 of the remaining 49, which were included for correlation. FNAC smears and cell blocks were examined for cytomorphology, immunohistochemistry (IHC) of EGFR and VEGF, and droplet digital PCR for EGFR mutations (T790M, L858R). data, and were Results: = most EGFR expression was in and VEGF in EGFR mutations were with cases EGFR and VEGF expression significantly correlated (P = and were with poor therapy = = and = = Conclusion: EGFR and VEGF are in metastatic OSCC and poor and outcomes. FNAC is a practical for IHC and supporting its in evaluation. OF OF CYTOLOGY Department of Pathology, Pradesh, India Background: smear screening is in screening and also in and parameter